1
00:00:03,550 --> 00:00:05,371
One dollar and fifty cents.

2
00:00:06,128 --> 00:00:10,491
In 1985, that could buy you a
hot dog and a soda at Costco.

3
00:00:11,172 --> 00:00:16,015
More than forty years later, the price
is still one dollar and fifty cents.

4
00:00:16,895 --> 00:00:19,419
Think about everything
that has changed. Rent.

5
00:00:19,459 --> 00:00:24,129
Wages. Food costs. The price of
almost everything around us.

6
00:00:24,568 --> 00:00:29,832
Yet one of America's largest retailers
keeps selling this combination for the same

7
00:00:29,872 --> 00:00:30,872
price.

8
00:00:31,093 --> 00:00:32,006
Why?

9
00:00:32,046 --> 00:00:34,675
The answer is bigger than a hot dog.

10
00:00:36,434 --> 00:00:40,243
Keeping a price unchanged
for four decades is unusual.

11
00:00:40,774 --> 00:00:44,397
Inflation means the same
dollar buys less over time.

12
00:00:45,007 --> 00:00:49,967
Businesses normally respond by raising
prices, reducing costs, or changing what they

13
00:00:50,007 --> 00:00:50,934
sell.

14
00:00:50,974 --> 00:00:55,644
Costco chose a different path for its
most recognizable food-court item.

15
00:00:56,131 --> 00:01:00,491
The company protected the price while
adapting the operation behind it.

16
00:01:00,795 --> 00:01:05,567
What makes a cheap hot dog valuable to a
retailer selling hundreds of billions of

17
00:01:05,607 --> 00:01:06,637
dollars in merchandise?

18
00:01:08,706 --> 00:01:12,549
To understand the hot dog, you
first have to understand Costco.

19
00:01:13,241 --> 00:01:18,284
Its warehouses are enormous. Products
are frequently sold in bulk.

20
00:01:18,792 --> 00:01:23,685
The shelves are practical, the presentation
is restrained, and the assortment is

21
00:01:23,725 --> 00:01:24,955
deliberately limited.

22
00:01:25,535 --> 00:01:30,441
Instead of convincing customers to pay
more for an elaborate shopping experience,

23
00:01:30,511 --> 00:01:34,041
Costco works to convince them they're
getting more for their money.

24
00:01:35,641 --> 00:01:39,032
Most shoppers pay Costco before
they buy their groceries.

25
00:01:39,765 --> 00:01:41,732
They purchase an annual membership.

26
00:01:42,315 --> 00:01:46,848
Costco must give people a reason to
join, and then a reason to renew.

27
00:01:47,351 --> 00:01:51,139
Every visit becomes an
opportunity to demonstrate value.

28
00:01:51,539 --> 00:01:54,431
Low prices aren't simply
a promotional message.

29
00:01:54,901 --> 00:01:58,811
They're part of the promise customers
expect their membership to deliver.

30
00:02:00,632 --> 00:02:06,327
In fiscal 2025, Costco reported more
than five point three billion dollars in

31
00:02:06,367 --> 00:02:07,777
membership fee revenue.

32
00:02:08,186 --> 00:02:13,433
Its renewal rate in the United States
and Canada was over ninety-two percent.

33
00:02:13,738 --> 00:02:18,151
That revenue supports the overall
economics, although it doesn't mean every

34
00:02:18,191 --> 00:02:20,521
membership dollar is pure profit.

35
00:02:20,951 --> 00:02:25,507
The retailer has a powerful incentive to
protect the value customers believe they're

36
00:02:25,547 --> 00:02:26,547
receiving.

37
00:02:27,838 --> 00:02:32,553
Costco doesn't try to offer every
possible version of every product.

38
00:02:33,089 --> 00:02:38,568
By concentrating demand on a smaller
assortment, it can purchase greater volumes of

39
00:02:38,608 --> 00:02:39,818
individual items.

40
00:02:40,430 --> 00:02:45,754
That can strengthen negotiating power,
simplify inventory and reduce handling costs.

41
00:02:46,654 --> 00:02:51,234
The important lesson is that low
prices aren't created at the checkout.

42
00:02:51,839 --> 00:02:56,000
They're created through thousands of
decisions made before a product reaches the

43
00:02:56,040 --> 00:02:57,040
shelf.

44
00:02:58,309 --> 00:03:01,431
But the hot dog presents
a special challenge.

45
00:03:01,951 --> 00:03:06,769
Unlike a television or a box of detergent,
it has to be prepared and served.

46
00:03:07,341 --> 00:03:12,282
There are ingredients, equipment, labor,
packaging and drinks to consider.

47
00:03:12,745 --> 00:03:15,155
And over time, those costs change.

48
00:03:15,896 --> 00:03:20,179
Instead, it focused on controlling
the operation behind the meal.

49
00:03:21,901 --> 00:03:26,635
Costco has changed parts of the hot dog
operation while keeping the headline price.

50
00:03:27,535 --> 00:03:32,179
It moved toward its own Kirkland Signature
hot dogs, giving the company more control

51
00:03:32,219 --> 00:03:34,709
over sourcing and production.

52
00:03:35,133 --> 00:03:38,917
Stronger buying power helps
Costco keep costs down.

53
00:03:39,382 --> 00:03:42,522
None of this means the combo
is immune to inflation.

54
00:03:43,197 --> 00:03:48,045
It means the company has repeatedly chosen
to defend the customer-facing price by

55
00:03:48,085 --> 00:03:50,975
working on the economics behind it.

56
00:03:52,538 --> 00:03:56,807
Customers cannot memorize the price
of every product in a warehouse.

57
00:03:57,557 --> 00:04:00,221
But they can remember one
dollar and fifty cents.

58
00:04:01,042 --> 00:04:06,060
That makes the hot dog an unusually visible
symbol of Costco's pricing philosophy.

59
00:04:06,708 --> 00:04:11,761
Every time a shopper sees that familiar
price, the company reinforces a simple

60
00:04:11,801 --> 00:04:12,801
message.

61
00:04:13,031 --> 00:04:14,775
We're still trying to give you value.

62
00:04:15,396 --> 00:04:18,627
The hot dog doesn't prove
every item is the cheapest.

63
00:04:19,126 --> 00:04:22,644
It makes the promise of
value easier to recognize.

64
00:04:24,550 --> 00:04:29,101
Think about what happens when a company
keeps a familiar promise for decades.

65
00:04:29,817 --> 00:04:34,620
They may come to expect consistency,
especially from a retailer whose business

66
00:04:34,660 --> 00:04:37,970
depends on repeat visits
and membership renewals.

67
00:04:38,319 --> 00:04:41,786
The hot dog is one highly visible
example of that consistency.

68
00:04:42,490 --> 00:04:45,136
Costco's membership
performance is measurable.

69
00:04:45,750 --> 00:04:51,010
The exact contribution of the hot dog to
renewals is not publicly established.

70
00:04:51,512 --> 00:04:56,434
The connection is a strategic interpretation,
not a proven dollar-for-dollar return.

71
00:04:58,239 --> 00:05:02,062
The hot dog is a tiny part of
an enormous retail operation.

72
00:05:02,713 --> 00:05:08,725
In fiscal 2025, Costco reported nearly two
hundred and seventy billion dollars in

73
00:05:08,765 --> 00:05:09,765
net sales.

74
00:05:10,092 --> 00:05:15,059
Its model depends on moving large quantities
of merchandise through a disciplined

75
00:05:15,099 --> 00:05:16,489
warehouse network.

76
00:05:16,976 --> 00:05:22,536
It does mean Costco evaluates its business
as a system, not simply as a collection

77
00:05:22,576 --> 00:05:24,366
of isolated price tags.

78
00:05:26,146 --> 00:05:31,767
So why doesn't every retailer simply
copy the one-dollar-fifty-cent hot dog?

79
00:05:32,221 --> 00:05:34,383
Because copying a price is easy.

80
00:05:34,758 --> 00:05:37,723
Copying the system supporting
it is much harder.

81
00:05:38,224 --> 00:05:42,625
A competitor would need its own purchasing
advantages, efficient operations,

82
00:05:43,335 --> 00:05:46,725
customer relationships and
reasons for shoppers to return.

83
00:05:47,272 --> 00:05:52,416
Discounting one item without those
advantages may simply reduce earnings.

84
00:05:52,463 --> 00:05:56,857
It's that the price fits within a broader
business model built to deliver value

85
00:05:56,897 --> 00:05:57,897
consistently.

86
00:05:59,527 --> 00:06:03,842
Costco does raise prices when its
broader economics require it.

87
00:06:04,159 --> 00:06:09,780
In September 2024, it increased annual
membership fees in the United States and

88
00:06:09,820 --> 00:06:10,820
Canada.

89
00:06:10,919 --> 00:06:15,342
Yet the famous hot dog combo remained
at one dollar and fifty cents.

90
00:06:16,159 --> 00:06:19,988
The company is not committed to
freezing every price forever.

91
00:06:20,637 --> 00:06:25,848
The hot dog appears to occupy a special
place in how Costco communicates value.

92
00:06:27,587 --> 00:06:31,913
It's tempting to call the hot dog a
loss leader and leave the story there.

93
00:06:32,318 --> 00:06:35,267
Does Costco actually lose
money on every combo?

94
00:06:35,830 --> 00:06:39,953
The company's public financial reports
don't give us a precise answer.

95
00:06:40,378 --> 00:06:44,357
We can document the price. We can
examine its supply chain decisions.

96
00:06:44,927 --> 00:06:48,077
And we can measure Costco's
broader financial performance.

97
00:06:48,872 --> 00:06:55,150
What we cannot responsibly do is invent a
per-hot-dog loss and present it as fact.

98
00:06:55,732 --> 00:06:59,179
The real strategy is interesting
enough without that claim.

99
00:07:00,905 --> 00:07:05,237
The most valuable part of this story
may not be the hot dog itself.

100
00:07:05,741 --> 00:07:09,155
It may be the expectation
the price helps reinforce.

101
00:07:09,742 --> 00:07:14,293
A customer enters Costco believing
the company is serious about value.

102
00:07:14,649 --> 00:07:19,113
The membership model gives Costco a
reason to keep earning that belief.

103
00:07:19,682 --> 00:07:22,074
The hot dog is a symbol of those choices.

104
00:07:22,822 --> 00:07:26,005
Small enough to remember.
Familiar enough to notice.

105
00:07:26,435 --> 00:07:29,285
And unusual enough to make people ask why.

106
00:07:30,951 --> 00:07:35,834
So why does Costco refuse to raise
the price of its famous hot dog?

107
00:07:36,290 --> 00:07:41,656
It demonstrates a commitment to recognizable
value, supported by purchasing scale,

108
00:07:41,926 --> 00:07:46,396
operating discipline and a membership
business that rewards customer loyalty.

109
00:07:46,968 --> 00:07:50,324
Costco hasn't frozen the
entire business in 1985.

110
00:07:51,194 --> 00:07:55,293
It has adapted the business while
protecting one extraordinary price.

111
00:07:55,896 --> 00:08:00,987
Sometimes the clearest evidence of a company's
strategy isn't found in a boardroom.

112
00:08:01,340 --> 00:08:03,305
It's printed above a hot dog.

113
00:08:03,780 --> 00:08:05,882
Every brand leaves evidence.

